From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] windows.ui.xaml: ResourceDictionary, Style, Setter -- resources and styles

Programs style their pages and controls with resources and styles; there were none. ResourceDictionary (an IMap<Object,Object>: Lookup, HasKey, Insert, Remove, Clear; MergedDictionaries; ThemeDictionaries by the current theme; Source not loaded yet), FrameworkElement.Resources and Application.Resources; Style (Setters, TargetType, BasedOn, Seal) and Setter (Property, Value); a style's values sit below an element's own values; an explicit Style applies its setters (BasedOn's first), an element without one gets the implicit style for its type (a style keyed by its TargetType, ours or the application's) when first laid out. Resources are found from an element up its parents, then the application's, then the desktop's own theme resources: the brushes, sizes, fonts and corner radii programs ask for by their usual names (TextFillColorPrimaryBrush, ControlStrokeColorDefaultBrush, ControlCornerRadius ...) made from the desktop's look -- its system colours, square for Classic, rounded for Rounded -- not Microsoft's. Markup: x:Key, keyed and implicit resources in dictionaries and Resources property elements, MergedDictionaries, ThemeDictionaries, Style TargetType with prefixes, Setter Property/Value, {StaticResource}, {ThemeResource}, {x:Null}. Interface identity per windows-rs 0.30.0 (MIT); the implementation is ours. Behind the XAML switch of 6131.
---
